-- status  : Proved
-- author  : @WillR
-- created : 2026-09-28T01:39:30.19791+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/7a057114-f8e1-4c51-8363-1114b06d0d66
-- title:
--   With a positive definite $H_0$: one step of the $A$-conjugacy of the PCG search directions, $d_{k+1}^T A d_k = 0$
-- statement:
--   Let $f(x) = \tfrac12 x^TAx + b^Tx$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ with $A$ symmetric positive definite, and run the preconditioned conjugate gradient method with a **fixed positive definite** preconditioner $H_0$ and exact line searches from $x_0$, so that with $g_k = Ax_k + b$ and $y_k = g_{k+1} - g_k$ the directions obey
--
--   $$d_{k+1} = -H_0 g_{k+1} + \beta_k\, d_k, \qquad \beta_k = \frac{y_k^T H_0 g_{k+1}}{y_k^T d_k}.$$
--
--   Then two successive search directions are $A$-conjugate:
--
--   $$d_{k+1}^T A\, d_k = 0 \qquad \text{for every } k \ge 0.$$
--
--   **Why it is true.** The gradient of a quadratic is affine, so along the line $x + a\,d$ the gradient changes by $a\,Ad$. Since $y_k = g_{k+1} - g_k$ is exactly the change produced by the step $a_k = \mathrm{exactStep}$, one has $y_k = a_k\,A d_k$, and therefore
--
--   $$\beta_k = \frac{(A d_k)^T (H_0 g_{k+1})}{(A d_k)^T d_k},$$
--
--   the factor $a_k$ cancelling. Substituting the direction recurrence and using that $u^TAv = v^TAu$ for symmetric $A$,
--
--   $$d_{k+1}^T A d_k = -(H_0 g_{k+1})^T (A d_k) + \beta_k \, (d_k^T A d_k) = -\,\frac{(A d_k)^T (H_0 g_{k+1})}{(A d_k)^T d_k}\,(A d_k)^T d_k + (A d_k)^T (H_0 g_{k+1}) = 0.$$
--
--   **Why the positive-definiteness hypothesis is needed, and not cosmetic.** The cancellation divides by $a_k$. If $H_0$ is merely symmetric, the case $a_k = 0$ with $d_k \ne 0$ genuinely occurs: for $n = 2$, $A = I$, $H_0 = \mathrm{diag}(1,-1)$, $b = (1,1)$, $x_0 = 0$, one gets $d_0 = (-1,1)$ with $g_0^T d_0 = 0$, hence $a_0 = 0$, hence $y_0 = 0$ and $\beta_0 = 0/0 = 0$, so $d_1 = d_0$ and $d_1^T A d_0 = 2 \ne 0$. If $H_0$ is positive definite then $a_k = 0$ forces $g_k^T H_0 g_k = 0$, hence $g_k = 0$, hence $d_k = 0$, and the claim is vacuous. So the hypothesis removes exactly the obstructing case.
--
--   This is a statement about one index only, so unlike the full conjugacy it is a direct algebraic consequence of the definition of $\beta$ and requires no induction.
-- source:
--   Nocedal, Updating Quasi-Newton Matrices with Limited Storage, Math. Comp. 35 (1980), p. 777, iteration (13) and the A-conjugacy of the search directions underlying eqs. (15)-(16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LimitedBFGS_SQN_pcgIter

open Matrix

namespace LimitedBFGS.SQN

/-- One step of the `A`-conjugacy of the search directions generated by the
preconditioned conjugate gradient iteration (13) of Nocedal 1980, p. 777, when the fixed
preconditioner `H0` is **positive definite**.

Write `st_k = pcgIter A b H0 x0 k` and `st_{k+1} = pcgIter A b H0 x0 (k + 1)`.
The direction recurrence is `d_{k+1} = -H0 g_{k+1} + beta_k d_k` with
`beta_k = y_kᵀ H0 g_{k+1} / y_kᵀ d_k` and `y_k = g_{k+1} - g_k`. Then

    d_{k+1}ᵀ A d_k = 0.

**Proof.** The gradient of a quadratic is affine, so along the line `x + a d` the gradient
changes by `a (A *ᵥ d)`. Since `y_k = g_{k+1} - g_k` is exactly the change produced by the
line-search step `a_k = exactStep A b st_k.x st_k.d`, one has `y_k = a_k (A *ᵥ d_k)`, and
therefore

    beta_k = [(A d_k)ᵀ (H0 g_{k+1})] / [(A d_k)ᵀ d_k],

the factor `a_k` cancelling. Substituting the direction recurrence and using that
`uᵀ A v = vᵀ A u` for symmetric `A`,

    d_{k+1}ᵀ A d_k = -(H0 g_{k+1})ᵀ (A d_k) + beta_k (d_kᵀ A d_k)
                  = -[(A d_k)ᵀ (H0 g_{k+1})] + [(A d_k)ᵀ (H0 g_{k+1})] = 0.

**Why `H0` must be positive definite.** The cancellation above divides by
`a_k`, and is legitimate only when `a_k ≠ 0`. For a merely symmetric `H0` the case
`a_k = 0` with `d_k ≠ 0` really occurs: then `y_k = 0`, so the defining denominator
`y_kᵀ d_k` vanishes while the numerator need not, and the identity is false. Positive
definiteness removes that case, because `a_k = 0` forces `g_kᵀ H0 g_k = 0`, which for `H0`
positive definite forces `g_k = 0`, hence `d_k = 0`, and the claim is then vacuous.

This is the corrected form of `pcg_direction_A_conjugacy_step`, which omits the
positive-definiteness hypothesis on `H0` and is therefore false; the counterexample is
`n = 2`, `A = I`, `H0 = diag(1, -1)`, `b = (1, 1)`, `x0 = 0`, `k = 0`, for which
`d_1ᵀ A d_0 = 2`. -/
theorem pcg_direction_A_conjugacy_step_pd {n : ℕ} (A : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (hA : A.PosDef)
    (b : Fin n → ℝ) (H₀ : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (hH₀ : H₀.PosDef) (x₀ : Fin n → ℝ) (k : ℕ) :
    (pcgIter A b H₀ x₀ (k + 1)).d ⬝ᵥ (A *ᵥ (pcgIter A b H₀ x₀ k).d) = 0 := by sorry

end LimitedBFGS.SQN
